# SET AT MINT TIME, NOT BEFORE. Three fields here can only be correct on the day of release,
# and a guessed value in a citation record is a permanent wrong answer:
#
#   version:        the dated release tag being minted, e.g. release-2026-09-21.
#                   NOT vN.N.N -- v1/v2/v3 already name the three intervention rungs in
#                   published vocabulary, and a version-numbered tag collides with them in
#                   the one artifact that can never be corrected. See VERSIONING.md.
#   doi:            the Zenodo CONCEPT doi, which always resolves to the newest release --
#                   not the per-version doi. Reserve it in Zenodo before the first Release so
#                   it can be written here, rather than added in a follow-up.
#   date-released:  the actual release date. It read 2026-09-20 for a day, which was the date
#                   an earlier title was settled and not a release of anything.
#
# Every figure below is recomputed by `check_citation.py` against the live corpus and the
# release is refused if one has moved, because this file is the only place in the repository
# where "it was true when I wrote it" is not a defence.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this dataset, the instrument, or the floor methodology, please cite it.
title: >-
  No position, only consensus: what political instruments actually measure in
  language models
authors:
  - family-names: Gorrie
    given-names: Ian
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-3531-0655"
    affiliation: Independent researcher
abstract: >-
  A forced-choice political instrument of 32 author-written propositions, arranged as 16
  mirrored pairs, administered to a panel of frontier and open-weight language models under
  a bare baseline, a balance instruction, a content-free placebo and a commitment condition.

  Instruments of this class report where a model sits on a political axis. That coordinate is
  not stable at the resolution the field reports it. Measured on the same sheets with the same
  estimator, the balance instruction moves position by a median of 0.131 and reprinting the
  same propositions in a different order moves it by a median of 0.088 — so the instruction is
  larger on the typical model, and it falls well inside the order floor's p90 of 0.281, which
  is to say inside what reordering alone produces on the noisiest models. Forty-three per cent
  of order pairs are themselves significant under the estimator. An effect of that size cannot
  be attributed to the instruction unless the floor is reported beside it, per model rather
  than pooled. Of fourteen published studies audited against fourteen methodological controls,
  not one reports such a floor as a distribution; the closest, Törnberg and Schimmel (2026),
  reports its centre and spread and stops short of an upper percentile.

  What is stable is agreement. Across the contested normative propositions in the bank the
  panel agrees 97.1% of the time, against 99.0% for documented matters of record, and that
  consensus holds across vendors and jurisdictions and survives abliteration — all five builds
  with the refusal direction projected out of their weights agree with every normative
  proposition in the bank. Five is a thin class and the result is exploratory, computed after
  the data was seen in answer to an objection. It is therefore not installed by safety tuning,
  though shared pretraining is not ruled out. The measurable property of this panel is not where a model stands, but that the
  panel stands together.

  The emphasis runs against the evidence. Models use their strongest available answer on 42.5%
  of contested normative claims and 36.6% of documented matters of record, 36 models to 14,
  sign test p = 0.0026 — they commit hardest where the record is thinnest. Two confounds are
  reported with it rather than around it. Claim type is near-collinear with specificity: of
  the twenty generic items eighteen are normative, and every jurisdiction-tagged item is
  documented, so "has a record behind it" and "names a country" are hard to separate here.
  And the panel is not fifty-six independent systems: it contains quantisation pairs,
  snapshot pairs and abliterated builds sharing base weights, which this study elsewhere
  calls same-version variants. The direction is reported; the cause is not isolated and the
  unit is not fully independent.

  Every floor is reported with a minimum detectable effect, every estimator with a
  false-positive rate measured against a null built by splitting real cells in half, and
  every figure with the command that produces it.
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/gorrie/bias-study"
keywords:
  - large language models
  - measurement validity
  - noise floor
  - forced-choice instruments
  - presentation order
  - political bias evaluation
